          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.214,
            0.375,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Urban Heat Island Effect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.234,
            0.616,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cities are warmer than their surrounding rural areas, especially during summer and at night. Compared to rural areas, cities have more built-up areas, including buildings of steel, concrete, brick, and roads made up of concrete and asphalt. All these materials absorb solar radiation and retain heat. The re-radiated heat from these materials warms cities more than the surrounding rural areas. This increases energy demand for air conditioning further stressing the urban ecosystems. Rural areas and forests have more vegetation, and stay cool through shade and plant transpiration. The urban heat island effect shows how human land use can alter the local climate (Fig. 13.6)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.627,
            0.21,
            0.929,
            0.413
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.627,
                0.21,
                0.929,
                0.413
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.627,
                0.419,
                0.928,
                0.435
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 13.6: Urban heat island effect in a city"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.469,
            0.775,
            0.538
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The amount of radiation received by a particular region on the Earth’s surface also depends on its latitude. The uneven heating due to the latitude of a place is important as it determines the atmospheric conditions of that place."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.546,
            0.481,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.1.2 Latitude and Earth’s shape"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.568,
            0.775,
            0.705
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have already learnt in earlier grades that the Earth is spherical. As a result, the Sun’s rays strike different latitudes at different angles on the Earth. The Sun’s radiation falling on the equatorial region is concentrated over a smaller area, where it is spread over a larger area in the polar region. This is why, equatorial regions remain relatively warm throughout the year, whereas polar regions experience much colder conditions. This uneven heating creates temperature differences between the equator and the poles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.788,
            0.579,
            0.893,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Grade 7 Exploring Society: India and Beyond Part 1 Chapter 3",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.707,
            0.776,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have also learnt how the Earth’s spherical shape and the tilt of the Earth’s axis of rotation give rise to seasons, and the changing length of daytime during one revolution of the Earth around the Sun. Thus, solar radiation is not evenly distributed across the globe. This uneven heating of the Earth’s surface across the globe drives global winds and ocean currents."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.788,
            0.713,
            0.854,
            0.746
          ],
